WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Git automation often handles more than a simple source tree.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:15.320
Repositories may include submodules, large files, generated artifacts, release bundles, vendored dependencies, scripts, configuration files, and metadata.

3
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.220
Each of these can influence later automation.

4
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:25.360
Treat repository content as input until it has passed the expected review and trust path.

5
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.960
Submodules create additional repository and commit trust relationships.

6
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:36.220
A main repository may be reviewed, but a submodule points to another repository and a specific commit.

7
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.840
Automation should understand whether submodules are expected, which sources are approved, and whether the pinned commits are part of the reviewed change.

8
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.140
Blindly updating or trusting submodules can expand the trust boundary without making it obvious.

9
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:55.820
Large-file workflows and generated artifacts can introduce storage, integrity, and review concerns.

10
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.600
Generated release bundles may not be easy to inspect.

11
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.820
Large files may be pulled from separate storage.

12
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.920
Artifacts may carry credentials, build outputs, logs, or generated packages.

13
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:19.660
Automation should be clear about which content is source, which content is generated, which content is trusted, and which content is allowed to move into release or deployment stages.

14
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.460
Workspace hygiene matters.

15
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:25.240
Data from one job should not contaminate another.

16
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:34.940
Secrets, credentials, generated packages, sensitive logs, and temporary files should not be committed, archived, or uploaded accidentally as artifacts.

17
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.680
Cleanup should be safe and scoped, with job isolation where possible.

18
00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:46.260
Git automation is safer when every input and output has a known purpose and a controlled lifecycle.
